{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 30,
    "dDurationMs": 5999,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most processes in our universe are time reversible in other words the physics works the same way forwards or backwards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6580,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is why you can't tell if I'm playing these videos normally or in reverse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11740,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People typically point to entropy as the only exception to this rule. The second law of thermodynamics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17650,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states that the entropy of a system or the amount of disorder always increases with time *buzzer sound*"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24220,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But increasing entropy is an emergent property, the result of the motions of many many particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29859,
    "dDurationMs": 1891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which raises the question:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31750,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can fundamental particles themselves tell the direction of time?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35000,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, in other words, is there a physical process on the scale of individual particles that looks different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41320,
    "dDurationMs": 5089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forwards vs backwards? The answer surprisingly is yes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46780,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In particle physics there are three major symmetries that were always expected to hold: charge, parity, and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53640,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time symmetry, as we've already discussed, means interactions work the same way forwards or backwards in time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59800,
    "dDurationMs": 5329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Charge symmetry means interactions are unaffected if all the charges are swapped in other words"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65129,
    "dDurationMs": 7071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is nothing special about what we call positive charge. Nature treats it exactly equal and opposite to negative charge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72460,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and parity symmetry means the laws of physics are indifferent to left- or right-handedness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78000,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now to understand what that means imagine a giant mirror were held up to our universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and in the mirror the z direction Is reversed and my right hand becomes my left hand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89720,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the laws of physics shouldn't care I mean"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they should work exactly the same way in the mirror world as they do in the regular one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97620,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with no preference for left- or right-handedness or to put it another way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101260,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there should be no experiment that you could do that would tell you whether or not you are in the mirror world"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106960,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each of these symmetries is known by its initials- C, P, and T"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111260,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the 1950s it was thought that all fundamental particles obey these symmetries"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115920,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then in 1956 a paper written by Li and Yang pointed out that parity symmetry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121160,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had actually never been tested in experiments involving the weak force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124800,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So at Christmas break that year physics professor Chien Shang Wu of Columbia University had planned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130420,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to go on vacation with her husband who is also a physicist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133180,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but instead, so intrigued by the possibility that the weak force might violate parity, she decided to stay behind"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139719,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And be the first person to test it. To do this she and a team of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144280,
    "dDurationMs": 7129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Low-temperature scientists cooled a collection of cobalt-60 atoms to just three thousandths of a degree above absolute zero"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152170,
    "dDurationMs": 5419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then they applied a strong magnetic field to align all the nuclei with their spin pointing in the same direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157989,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now cobalt 60 is radioactive and it decays via the weak nuclear force releasing a beta particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163299,
    "dDurationMs": 6139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is just an electron and what the experiment measured was the direction in which these electrons were emitted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169750,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Relative to the spin of the cobalt 60 nuclei. To see how this would work under parity symmetry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174849,
    "dDurationMs": 6559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's consider the mirror image version of this experiment in the mirror the direction of the z axis is flipped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181409,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the direction of nuclear spin is not that's because an object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184950,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's rotating clockwise is still rotating clockwise in the mirror"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189280,
    "dDurationMs": 7039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this means that the spins of the regular and mirror nuclei are aligned the mirror experiment is actually the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196629,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Experiment as the original now when a cobalt 60 nucleus decays and emits an electron that electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202060,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Could go say to the left or to the right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204519,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now if parity symmetry is respected the electrons should be equally likely to go in any direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209799,
    "dDurationMs": 6619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That way both the normal and mirror image experiments would give the same results however if the electrons were emitted in one direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216909,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preferentially say in the positive z direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219510,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219930,
    "dDurationMs": 3089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then in the mirror experiment the electrons would also have to fly off"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223209,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preferentially in the mirror positive Z direction which is opposite to the original positive z direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228729,
    "dDurationMs": 5779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in the normal experiment the electrons would be emitted say opposite the direction of nuclear spin, but in the mirror version"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234509,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They'd be emitted in the same direction as nuclear spin, so how does this make any sense it would allow you to determine?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240989,
    "dDurationMs": 1990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether you're in the mirror world or not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242979,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's like the spinning top from Inception if the electrons from the cobalt 60 nuclei"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247650,
    "dDurationMs": 2729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Go one way, you're in mirror world and if they go the other way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250449,
    "dDurationMs": 6709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know you're in normal world now crazy as this may seem. This is exactly what professor Wu saw"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257769,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons were emitted preferentially in one direction and not just by a little bit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262500,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They were predominantly emitted opposite nuclear spin, so not only does the weak force violate parity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268380,
    "dDurationMs": 3959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It comes close to violating parity as much as is physically possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272620,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This destroyed a basic assumption of theoretical physics that had been around for decades"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277810,
    "dDurationMs": 6139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Somehow the universe cares about left or right-handed nests when wu announced her results"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284020,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They shocked the physics world after being told of the experiment famous physicist and Nobel laureate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290310,
    "dDurationMs": 7709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wolfgang Pauli said \"That's total nonsense,\" and insisted the result was mistaken when the experiment was independently replicated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298270,
    "dDurationMs": 7940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Theoretical physicists had to accept that the universe we live in is not the one they had imagined the Nobel Prize was actually awarded for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306210,
    "dDurationMs": 5429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The discovery of parity violation in 1957 the very same year these results were published"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312310,
    "dDurationMs": 6589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This required a profound shift in thinking for physicists, but before throwing everything out and starting again from scratch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318900,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They formulated a workaround"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321159,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe it was okay that the weak force broke parity because it's not a real"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326169,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "symmetry of the universe itself just part of a larger symmetry charge parity or CP symmetry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332380,
    "dDurationMs": 3619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea was if the mirror flipped not only these axes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336000,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it also swapped the particles for anti particles with their charges reversed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340270,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340770,
    "dDurationMs": 7739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the symmetry would be restored and the mathematics behind our laws of physics would still work now this gave physicists some comfort until in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349180,
    "dDurationMs": 5479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1964 it was found that some particles can also violate the combined charge parity symmetry and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355030,
    "dDurationMs": 7910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Boom you got yourself another Nobel Prize now two rules which physicists once thought were fundamental laws of nature were broken so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363219,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They retreated behind their last set of theoretical defenses the combined symmetry of CP T where T is time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370870,
    "dDurationMs": 3079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure, they said the weak force violates parity and charge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374500,
    "dDurationMs": 7489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Parity, but certainly not charge parity and time together and to this day physicists are still pretty sure that CP T is a real"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382030,
    "dDurationMs": 7189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Symmetry of the universe so far no experiment has found a violation of CPT in fact if CP T is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389500,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Violated we would have to rewrite a lot of the last century's work because it would mean that special relativity and quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395289,
    "dDurationMs": 2540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Field theory are both wrong ok"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397830,
    "dDurationMs": 1619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let's say that CP T"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399449,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is a true symmetry think about the implications of this if we know that CP can be violated?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404669,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But CP t cannot, then time symmetry must also be broken"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409120,
    "dDurationMs": 6589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "otherwise there would be no way for the combined three-way symmetry to be maintained while two of the sub symmetries are broken and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416650,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists have actually conducted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418180,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Experiments that confirm that certain particles directly break time symmetry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422740,
    "dDurationMs": 6289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example when a pair of quarks are held together by the strong force there are sometimes two different possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429249,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Arrangements and they can switch back and forth between these two arrangements via the weak force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434139,
    "dDurationMs": 2869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But switching in one direction takes longer than switching back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437319,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you could make a recording of this event it would look different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440499,
    "dDurationMs": 6770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you played the recording forwards than if you played it backwards and that's exactly what it means to break time symmetry in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447789,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Certain cases then fundamental particles can tell the difference between going forwards and backwards in time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454180,
    "dDurationMs": 5929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second law of thermodynamics is not the only physical process that prefers one direction in time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460360,
    "dDurationMs": 7519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now is this the origin of our perception that time only goes one way, or is it the reason for the universe's arrow of time?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468460,
    "dDurationMs": 4309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The truth is that we still have no idea why time only goes in one direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473289,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What's interesting is that physicists once thought that parity charge and time were these symmetries that were unbreakable?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480180,
    "dDurationMs": 3869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But over time each of these symmetries was demonstrably violated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484389,
    "dDurationMs": 7549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is the ultimate symmetry CPT also unbreakable or will it fall taking quantum field theory and special relativity with it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492370,
    "dDurationMs": 6139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are just some of the big basic unresolved mysteries left in our quest to understand the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498969,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps one day another physicist will give up their vacation to figure out the answer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506919,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This episode of veritasium was animated and co-written by my friend jorge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510899,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cham who is also the creator of PhD comics and now he's written a book. Yeah. It's called we have no idea"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516630,
    "dDurationMs": 569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a book"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517199,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I wrote with physicist Daniel Weitz in' and it's about all the things we don't know about the universe although the big things like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522909,
    "dDurationMs": 1401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter and dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524310,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But also all the little things like what are all those fundamental particles for and it has cartoons in it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529420,
    "dDurationMs": 5369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yep, and lots of bad puns, so that's like. It's up your alley. You should check it out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534790,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'll put a link to it in the description and Olive core has other projects. Thanks"
    } ]
  } ]
}
